<Layout
  title="Networking & Subnetting Fundamentals"
  description="Understand IP addressing, subnet masks, CIDR notation, and routing well enough to design a subnet plan and troubleshoot connectivity issues with confidence."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container hero-content">
      <a class="back" href="/guides/">← Back to Guides</a>
      <span class="eyebrow">Networking · Guide</span>
      <h1>Networking &amp; Subnetting Fundamentals</h1>
      <p>
        Subnetting is arithmetic, not magic — once the binary clicks, you
        can design a network plan and troubleshoot connectivity issues
        without reaching for a calculator every time.
      </p>
      <div class="meta"><span>15 min read</span><span>·</span><span>Covers IPv4 — the concepts extend directly to IPv6</span></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="article-wrap">
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <h2>On this page</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#addresses">What an IP address actually is</a></li>
        <li><a href="#masks">Subnet masks and CIDR notation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sizing">Sizing a subnet for how many hosts you need</a></li>
        <li><a href="#planning">Planning a subnet layout</a></li>
        <li><a href="#routing">How routing decides where a packet goes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#private">Private ranges, NAT, and why they matter</a></li>
        <li><a href="#troubleshoot">Troubleshooting connectivity step by step</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="addresses">What an IP address actually is</h2>
    <p>
      An IPv4 address is a 32-bit number, almost always written as four
      decimal numbers from 0–255 separated by dots — for example
      <code>192.168.1.10</code>. Each of those four numbers is one byte
      (8 bits), so the whole address is 4 × 8 = 32 bits. Every device on a
      network needs a unique address within that network, the same way
      every house on a street needs a unique number.
    </p>
    <p>
      What makes subnetting confusing at first is that an IP address by
      itself doesn't tell you which part identifies the network and which
      part identifies the specific device. That's what the subnet mask is
      for.
    </p>

    <h2 id="masks">Subnet masks and CIDR notation</h2>
    <p>
      A subnet mask is also a 32-bit number, but instead of an address it
      describes a split point: a run of 1 bits for the network portion,
      followed by a run of 0 bits for the host portion. CIDR notation
      writes this as a slash and a number — the count of leading 1 bits —
      instead of writing out the whole mask.
    </p>
    <pre><code>10.0.1.0/24
  means the mask 255.255.255.0
  the first 24 bits identify the network
  the last 8 bits identify the host within it</code></pre>
    <p>
      With a /24, the first three numbers (24 bits = three full bytes) are
      the network, and the last number (8 bits) is the host — giving you
      256 possible values (0–255) for that last octet. Two of those are
      always reserved: the all-zeros address is the network address
      itself, and the all-ones address is the broadcast address. That
      leaves 254 usable addresses for actual devices in a /24.
    </p>
    <p>
      The general formula: a subnet with <code>h</code> host bits gives
      you 2 to the power of <code>h</code>, minus 2, usable addresses.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sizing">Sizing a subnet for how many hosts you need</h2>
    <p>
      Work backward from how many devices you actually need to fit, then
      round up to the nearest power of two:
    </p>
    <pre><code>/30 = 2 host bits  = 4 addresses,  2 usable  (point-to-point links)
/29 = 3 host bits  = 8 addresses,  6 usable
/28 = 4 host bits  = 16 addresses, 14 usable
/27 = 5 host bits  = 32 addresses, 30 usable
/26 = 6 host bits  = 64 addresses, 62 usable
/25 = 7 host bits  = 128 addresses, 126 usable
/24 = 8 host bits  = 256 addresses, 254 usable</code></pre>
    <p>
      A common mistake is sizing a subnet for exactly today's device
      count. Leave real headroom — a /24 that starts with 40 devices and
      grows steadily will run out of room eventually, and re-subnetting a
      live network later is far more disruptive than starting one size
      larger.
    </p>

    <h2 id="planning">Planning a subnet layout</h2>
    <p>
      When you subnet a larger block into smaller pieces, each subnet's
      network address is a multiple of its own size. Subnetting
      <code>10.0.0.0/16</code> into /24s gives you
      <code>10.0.0.0/24</code>, <code>10.0.1.0/24</code>,
      <code>10.0.2.0/24</code>, and so on — each one exactly 256
      addresses apart. This predictability is what makes CIDR notation
      genuinely usable for planning instead of just descriptive.
    </p>
    <p>
      A layout that scales well for most small-to-medium environments:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>One /24 (or larger) per environment or location — production, staging, each office site.</li>
      <li>Reserve the first handful of addresses in every subnet for gateways and infrastructure.</li>
      <li>Keep a written record of what's assigned where — an unmanaged IP plan is how two teams end up assigning the same address to different devices.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="routing">How routing decides where a packet goes</h2>
    <p>
      Every device keeps a routing table that answers one question for
      each outgoing packet: does the destination live on my local
      network, or does it need to go through a gateway? The device
      compares the destination address against its own subnet mask — if
      the destination falls within the same network, it sends the packet
      directly; otherwise, it forwards it to the default gateway, which
      repeats the same decision.
    </p>
    <pre><code>ip route
ip route get 8.8.8.8</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>ip route get</code> is the fastest way to answer "which
      interface and gateway will this specific destination actually use"
      without reading through the whole routing table by hand.
    </p>

    <h2 id="private">Private ranges, NAT, and why they matter</h2>
    <p>
      Three IPv4 ranges are reserved for private, non-internet-routable
      use — you'll see these constantly in home and corporate networks:
    </p>
    <pre><code>10.0.0.0/8       (10.0.0.0 - 10.255.255.255)
172.16.0.0/12    (172.16.0.0 - 172.31.255.255)
192.168.0.0/16   (192.168.0.0 - 192.168.255.255)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Devices using these addresses can't be reached directly from the
      public internet — a router performs Network Address Translation
      (NAT), rewriting outbound packets to use its own public address and
      tracking which internal device each response belongs to. This is
      exactly why two devices with the addresses
      <code>192.168.1.10/24</code> and <code>192.168.1.11/24</code> can
      reach each other directly, but two devices on different, unrelated
      <code>192.168.1.0/24</code> networks at two different homes can't —
      the address only has meaning within its own local network.
    </p>

    <h2 id="troubleshoot">Troubleshooting connectivity step by step</h2>
    <p>
      When a device can't reach something, work outward in layers instead
      of guessing:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ip addr show
ip route
ping &lt;gateway&gt;
ping 1.1.1.1
dig example.com</code></pre>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Confirm the device has a valid address and mask</strong> for the network it's on.</li>
      <li><strong>Ping the gateway.</strong> If this fails, the problem is local — a cable, a switch port, or a misconfigured address.</li>
      <li><strong>Ping a known-good public IP</strong> (not a hostname yet). If this fails but the gateway ping succeeded, the problem is upstream of your local network.</li>
      <li><strong>If the IP ping works but a hostname doesn't</strong>, the problem is DNS, not routing — don't waste time checking routes at this point.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Two devices on different subnets, like <code>192.168.10.10/24</code>
      and <code>192.168.11.10/24</code>, cannot talk directly no matter
      how "close" their addresses look — they're on entirely different
      /24 networks and need a router between them, by design.
    </p>

    <div class="related-tools">
      <h2>Related tools on this site</h2>
      <p>Put this guide into practice with these free tools:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/network/subnet-calculator">Subnet Calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/network/cidr-calculator">CIDR Calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/network/ip-calculator">IP Calculator</a></li>
        <li><a href="/network/dns-checker">DNS Checker</a></li>
        <li><a href="/interview-qa/#questions">Network Scenario Interview Q&amp;A</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>
